# Line endings: LF everywhere, including on Windows checkouts.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites
# every text file to CRLF as it is checked out. The repository content is fine -
# it is stored with LF - but the working tree on a Windows laptop is not, and
# the collector is a shell script destined for a Unix host. A bundle built from
# such a checkout would reach a client as a CRLF script and fail there with
# errors naming lines that look perfectly ordinary when opened.
#
# tools/make-release.sh refuses to package a script containing carriage returns,
# so this was caught rather than shipped. But the guard fired on every build
# attempt from a Windows machine, and the repair it suggests - stripping the
# carriage returns by hand - does not survive the next checkout. This file is
# the fix that does: "eol=lf" takes precedence over core.autocrlf, so a Windows
# clone gets LF without anyone having to change a global Git setting.
#
# Note that make-release.sh and the test suite are themselves shell scripts, so
# a CRLF checkout breaks the tooling as well as the payload.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files, declared rather than left to content detection. A .docx is a ZIP
# archive; any line-ending translation applied to it produces a file Word will
# not open.
*.docx binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.gz binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
